# Figma Design System

This project's design-system tool. Owns the reusable visual vocabulary — components, typography, colors, spacing, icons, logos — in the Design System file at **{{DESIGN_SYSTEM_FILE}}** (team **{{TEAM}}**, project **{{PROJECT}}**).

## Built on figma-use

This skill is an **overlay** on [figma-use](../figma-use/SKILL.md), Figma's official skill for driving the MCP server. `figma-use` owns the plugin-API mechanics — the `use_figma` tool, page-loading rules, font-loading recipes, query/set helpers, error recovery. This skill owns the project-specific *conventions* — what reusable pieces live in the design system, how they're organised, and when to republish.

**Whenever an operation here requires programmatic execution in Figma, follow `figma-use` for how to drive the API.** This skill never duplicates that material.

## Owned operations

- **read design tokens** — query Figma Variables for colors, typography, spacing, etc.
- **read design component** — query published components and their variants.
- **add design token** — add a new variable to the appropriate collection.
- **add design component** — add a new reusable component.
- **publish design library** — re-publish after changes with a descriptive note.

The design file **consumes** this library; it does not duplicate or detach components. Modifications happen here and propagate via library publishing.

## Access

All operations go through the **Figma MCP server**, driven via `figma-use`. Direct REST API calls or manual edits outside the MCP server are not permitted.

### Preflight (first operation of the session)

1. **Load figma-use.** Read [figma-use/SKILL.md](../figma-use/SKILL.md) before any `use_figma` call.
2. **Server health.** Verify the Figma MCP server is reachable and authenticated.
3. **Team / project access.** Verify the server can access team **{{TEAM}}** and project **{{PROJECT}}**.
4. **File access.** Verify file **{{DESIGN_SYSTEM_FILE}}** is reachable.

## File structure

The Design System file is the single source of truth for the product's visual vocabulary. The structure is **flat by default** — categories of reusable things, no hierarchy. Suggested pages:

- **Cover** — what this file is. Product name, status, last updated.
- **Components** — reusable visual elements (buttons, inputs, cards, nav, modal, etc.). Whatever gets used in more than one place. No atomic-hierarchy distinction — if it's reused, it's a component.
- **Typography** — the type system. Typeface(s), scale, weights, line heights, named styles per role.
- **Colors** — the palette and how it's used. Brand colors, semantic roles (surface, text, border, status), modes (light/dark) where applicable.
- **Spacing** — the rhythm. Base unit and scale, named values.
- **Icons** — the icon set and sizing rules.
- **Logos** — brand marks, lockups, and usage rules.

These are defaults, not mandates. If a project doesn't need a separate Logos page, fold it into Components. If it needs an Effects/Shadows or Motion page, add one. The principle: **a flat catalog of reusable building blocks, organised by category, not by abstraction level.**

## Conventions

### Components

- Built from the design tokens — no hardcoded colors, sizes, or typography.
- Use **Figma auto layout** for responsive behavior.
- Define meaningful **variants** via Figma component properties (size, style, state).
- Naming is `Category / Name / Variant` (e.g., `Buttons / Primary / Default`) — but the team picks the granularity that fits.
- All interactive components define the relevant states (Default, Hover, Active/Pressed, Focused, Disabled, Error where applicable).
- Build at the **real rendered sizes of the implemented product**, not assumed values — and verify tints/borders are actually visible (a white-on-white border reads as none; confirm with a screenshot, not the layer list).
- A **separate parallel component set** is a legitimate alternative to responsive variants/modes when existing components can't be cleanly retrofitted, or modes prove fragile — it isolates risk from already-approved designs. Prefer variants/variables; reach for a separate set deliberately.

### Tokens

- Implemented as **Figma Variables**.
- Names are semantically meaningful (e.g., `color.surface.primary`, `spacing.large`, `text.heading.lg`). Pick a convention the team likes and apply it consistently.
- Modes (light/dark) live as Figma Variable modes on the relevant collections, not as duplicate token sets.
- No mandated primitives/semantics/component-specific hierarchy — use what fits. Most projects don't need three layers.
- **Tints and overlays: store as pre-blended solid colors, not a base color at reduced opacity.** Paint-level opacity combined with a variable binding does *not* survive a library publish — instances in the consuming design file render fully opaque (an unreadable solid block). Bake the blend into a solid token (e.g., `color.tint.highlight`).
- Bind **mode-invariant** values (colors, radii equal across modes) freely. Values that **vary by mode** (spacing, border widths, sizes) only resolve correctly where the *consuming* surface sets its mode explicitly — so bind those only when the design file's pages/frames carry the right mode (see `figma-design-file`).

### Code map

Keep a checked-in **Figma↔code component map** in the consuming repo (e.g., `figma-code-map.json`) — a free stand-in for Figma Code Connect (which requires a paid Dev seat). When you **add, rename, or move** a component (in Figma or in code), update its entry: the code component name + path, and the Figma component name(s)/node(s) it corresponds to. A single code component may map from more than one Figma component (e.g., different representations or variant sets of the same thing). It is a **reference, not a sync** — it never edits code; it just lets design and dev point at each other.

### Library publishing

- Published as a Figma library to team **{{TEAM}}**.
- Re-publish after any token or component change, with a descriptive update note.
- Publishing is **separate from file version history.** Restoring a file's version does *not* revert what the library has already published — to roll back library-driven breakage, restore the design-system file **and re-publish**. Likewise, the design file renders from the **published** snapshot, not your local edits — re-publish before placing changed/new components there.

## Composition

When the `figma-design-file` skill is installed, the design file consumes this library. Changes propagate via republish — the design file never detaches instances or overrides locally; modifications round-trip through this skill.
